b-mcp-win-x64.zip\"},{\"url\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/Scormave/gramps-web-mcp/releases/assets/705417127\",\"id\":705417127,\"node_id\":\"RA_kwDOTDJ7sc4qC8-n\",\"name\":\"SHA256SUMS\",\"label\":\"\",\"uploader\":{\"login\":\"github-actions[bot]\",\"id\":41898282,\"node_id\":\"MDM6Qm90NDE4OTgyODI=\",\"avatar_url\":\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/15368?v=4\",\"gravatar_id\":\"\",\"url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/apps/github-actions\",\"followers_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/followers\",\"following_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}\",\"gists_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}\",\"starred_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}\",\"subscriptions_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions\",\"organizations_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/orgs\",\"repos_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/repos\",\"events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}\",\"received_events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/received_events\",\"type\":\"Bot\",\"user_view_type\":\"public\",\"site_admin\":false},\"content_type\":\"application/octet-stream\",\"state\":\"uploaded\",\"size\":1073,\"digest\":\"sha256:93ab904cb7cd0d13f02b4e5a6165dd92436b4eb9540d6ae6786d2cd725853022\",\"download_count\":2,\"created_at\":\"2026-11-03T11:48:38Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-11-03T11:48:38Z\",\"browser_download_url\":\"https://github.com/Scormave/gramps-web-mcp/releases/download/v2.9.1/SHA256SUMS\"}],\"tarball_url\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/Scormave/gramps-web-mcp/tarball/v2.9.1\",\"zipball_url\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/Scormave/gramps-web-mcp/zipball/v2.9.1\",\"body\":\"## Gramps Web MCP 2.9.1\\n\\n2.9.1 publishes the MCPB bundles and binary archives that 2.9.0 left out: three unit tests compared replies with Unix line endings and failed in the Windows build, which stopped the release files of every platform. The 2.9.0 Docker image and MCP Registry entry were published and work; the server is the same in both versions. These are the changes since 2.8.1.\\n\\nDuplicates, from finding to merging. `find_duplicates` lists the people entered twice across the tree, `create_person` and `create_records` refuse a person the tree already has, and `merge_objects` merges a duplicate into the record to keep with Gramps' own merge, which `undo_change` takes back. Names compare across spellings, Cyrillic and Latin letters, and the male and female surname forms of several languages. The new `audit-tree` prompt checks the tree, or one person's branch, for impossible dates, duplicates, unsourced facts and unlinked records. Name objects no longer lose fields without a word.\\n\\n### Added\\n\\n- **`merge_objects`** — Merges a duplicate into the record to keep, for people, families, events, places, sources, citations, notes, media and repositories, through Gramps Web's merge endpoint (API 3.15 or newer).\\n  - Gramps merges them in one transaction: the duplicate's details and every link to it move to the kept record, and the duplicate is deleted. The kept record keeps its own fields; a merged person keeps the other name as an alternate name.\\n  - Merging people also merges a family with the same partner unless `mergeFamilies` is false. Merging families merges their different fathers and mothers, keeping `fatherHandle` and `motherHandle` when given. Spouses, and a parent with their child, are refused before the user is asked.\\n  - The user confirms the merge in a dialog that names both records, as for `delete_object` and `undo_change`, and the reply gives the transaction that `undo_change` takes to restore the duplicate.\\n- **Duplicate check** — `create_person` and `create_records` check the tree for the new person first.\\n  - A namesake of the same gender and patronymic born up to two years apart, or a child of the same parents when there are no two years to compare, stops the call with `action: refused` and the possible duplicates; `create_records` then saves none of its records. `notDuplicateOf` names the people who are someone else and lets the person through.\\n  - Namesakes born up to five years apart, or without a year, are listed after creating, with a `merge_objects` hint for the new person. A namesake who died before the new person's birth year is someone else.\\n  - Names compare across spellings: old letters (ѣ, і, ѵ, ѳ, a final ъ), ё, doubled letters, -ия and -ья, Latin accents, and Cyrillic and Latin letters in English, Polish and Czech spelling (Novák, Nowak and Новак).\\n  - A surname matches its male, female and daughter's forms: Kowalski and Kowalska, -ов and -ова, Novák and Nováková, Hájek and Hájková, Nowak and Nowakówna, Kazlauskas, Kazlauskienė and Kazlauskaitė, Ozols and Ozola, Papadopoulos and Papadopoulou, Kovács and Kovácsné.\\n  - Given names are the same when the first word of one is a word of the other: Мария and Анна Мария, but not Иван Петрович and Петр Петрович.\\n- **`find_duplicates`** — Lists the people entered twice across the whole tree, with the comparison of the create check; namesakes who died more than five years apart are someone else.\\n  - People with a patronymic but no surname pair by given name and patronymic when their birth years or parents can be compared. Spouses, and a person and their parent, grandparent or earlier ancestor, are never paired.\\n  - Possible duplicates come first, each person with birth, death and parents, and the reply names `get_object` and `merge_objects` as the next steps. `personHandle` and `surname` narrow the pairs; `limit` and `offset` page through them.\\n  - It reads up to 20000 people, 500 per request, and changes nothing.\\n- **`audit-tree` prompt** — Checks the tree, or with `person` only that person's ancestors and descendants, and reports what it finds without changing anything:\\n  - impossible dates: a death before the birth, a parent born after a child or too young or old at a birth, a child born after a parent's death, events after a death, and a loop in the pedigree;\\n  - possible duplicates from `find_duplicates`;\\n  - people and events without citations;\\n  - people with no family links, and records that nothing links to;\\n  - missing names, gender, dates, places and coordinates.\\n\\n  It runs Gramps filter rules on the server through `list_objects`, reads timelines for the people it flags, orders the findings by check, most serious first, and offers fixes only for the user to confirm. `checks` runs only some of the checks.\\n\\n### Changed\\n\\n- **`create_family`** — The description says what the tool returns: the handle, Gramps ID and stored relationship type, then the `update_family` calls that add the children or events left out. It also says how the arguments work together: Married goes with a recorded marriage and its Marriage event in the Family role, each child carries its own relation to the father and to the mother, children keep the order given, and citations, notes, media, tags and attributes belong to the family itself.\\n- **Five more tool descriptions** say what they left out:\\n  - `update_family`: Gramps Web updates the father, mother and children in the same save, refuses a person who does not exist, and returns only a header.\\n  - `create_event`: when to write a range and when a span, that birth and baptism are two events, and that one participant's Age goes on their event reference.\\n  - `create_source`: which part of a reference goes into which argument, and what belongs on the citation.\\n  - `get_object`: an `objectType` that disagrees with the ID is rejected, and what `extended` adds.\\n  - `create_media`: it is the only tool that uploads a file, and where a transcription or a new file goes.\\n\\n### Fixed\\n\\n- **Name objects** in `primaryName` or `alternateNames` of `create_person`, `update_person` or `create_records` no longer lose fields without a word. `{\\\"first\\\": \\\"Jan\\\", \\\"surnameList\\\": [...]}` used to save a person named Jan with no surname, because only `first_name` and `surname_list` were read as native Gramps fields.\\n  - The native fields now work in camelCase too (`firstName`, `surnameList`, `originType`) and mix with the shortcuts `given`, `surname`, `patronymic` and the rest.\\n  - Any other field is refused with an error that names it and lists the accepted fields. So are two fields for the same thing, a whole-name `name` with `given` or `surname`, `prefix`, `connector` or `origin_type` without a surname, and an object with no name in it. The error reaches the agent instead of a bare \\\"An error occurred invoking\\\".\\n- **`alternateNames`** no longer skips an entry it cannot read. An object without a name, a number, or a stringified array with invalid JSON used to drop every alternate name; these are errors now.\\n- **`surname_list`** — In a list sent without `primary` flags, only the first surname is primary instead of every one. An entry with `primary: true` wins over the order.\\n- **`undo_change`** names the transaction by its description. Gramps Web's response for one transaction leaves the description out, so the dry run, the confirmation question and the reply said \\\"(no description)\\\", and the undo was saved as \\\"Undo transaction N\\\" without it. The description now comes from the history list: the one row by its number on Gramps Web API 3.21 or newer, the newest ten rows on older versions. When neither has it, \\\"(no description)\\\" stays.\\n\\n### Upgrade from 2.8.1\\n\\nNo settings to change. Clients see two new tools, 35 in all, and one new prompt, 8 in all. With `GRAMPS_READ_ONLY=true`, `merge_objects` is hidden like the other write tools; `find_duplicates` only reads and stays.\\n\\n**Older Gramps Web.** `merge_objects` needs Gramps Web API 3.15 or newer. On an older server it answers with an error that names the version, and the other tools work as before.\\n\\n**Confirmation.** `GRAMPS_CONFIRMATION` now covers `merge_objects` as well. With `required`, merges are refused in clients that cannot ask, and over stateless Streamable HTTP, as deletes and undos already are.\\n\\n**Scripts that create people.** `create_person` and `create_records` now stop on a likely duplicate with `action: refused`. Check the listed people with the user, then pass the ones who are someone else in `notDuplicateOf`, or update the existing person instead.\\n\\n**Name objects.** A name object with a field the server does not know now gets an error that names the field, instead of being saved without it.\\n\\n**Claude Desktop.** Install the new MCPB bundle and check that the extension is enabled afterwards.\\n\\n### Install\\n\\n**Docker**\\n\\n```bash\\ndocker pull ghcr.io/scormave/gramps-web-mcp:2.9.1\\n```\\n\\n**Claude Desktop** — Download the MCPB bundle for your platform from the assets below (`osx-arm64`, `osx-x64`, `win-x64`, `linux-x64`, or `linux-arm64`).\\n\\n**Other MCP clients without Docker** — Download the archive for your platform from the assets below and follow [Over stdio with the release binary](https://github.com/Scormave/gramps-web-mcp/blob/main/GrampsWeb.Mcp/docs/CLIENTS.md#over-stdio-with-the-release-binary).\\n\\n**From source** — Requires the .NET 10 SDK. Docker images, self-contained MCPB bundles and the binary archives include the runtime. A Release build needs a Six Labors license key, which ImageSharp 4 checks at compile time. The repository includes a free community key for the project's assemblies, so no setup is needed.\\n\\nEvery release file and image has a build provenance attestation; see [Releasing](https://github.com/Scormave/gramps-web-mcp/blob/main/GrampsWeb.Mcp/docs/RELEASING.md#post-release-checks) for the `gh attestation verify` commands.\\n\\n### Full changelog\\n\\nhttps://github.com/Scormave/gramps-web-mcp/compare/v2.8.1...v2.9.1\\n\"}"
}
